WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.493
[ORCHESTRA TUNING]

00:00:35.928 --> 00:00:39.421
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:08.363 --> 00:01:11.856
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:27.325 --> 00:01:30.818
[ORCHESTRA PLAYING]

00:01:38.802 --> 00:01:42.345
[CHOIR SINGING]

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:16.004
Good evening, ladies
and gentlemen.

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:22.420
I don't remember the exact
year, but in the summer

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:26.300
of the late '90s, I had a
chance meeting with John Oliver

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.240
at the time.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:30.820
I was the pianist for
the young artist chorus.

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:32.650
And John was invited
to do a master

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:35.650
class with our
talented young singers.

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:37.910
Apparently he liked my plan.

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:41.260
And after the class he
asked if I wanted a job,

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:43.690
I said sure, doing what?

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:46.700
He said I needed
a pianist at MIT.

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:48.010
I said great, doing what?

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:53.590
He said while there
are choirs at MIT.

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:56.340
There are choirs at MIT?

00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:00.820
And so for the next six years
I served as John's pianist

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:03.040
for the MIT Choirs.

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:06.610
When John retired in 1996,
I applied for the job

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:10.510
and here I am 22 years
later thanks to John.

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:13.856
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:21.710
John was an incredible mentor
for me during those six years.

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:25.490
And after he gained confidence
in my playing and my abilities,

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:28.490
he eventually hired me to be
his assistant at the Tanglewood

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:31.550
Festival Chorus, the chorus
to the Boston Symphony

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:33.830
which he also conducted.

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:37.490
I spent the next 15 years
having the incredible experience

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:39.920
of working with one
of the finest symphony

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:42.380
choruses in the
country, learning

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:45.560
even more about the
professional world of music

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:48.680
making under the
guidance of John Oliver.

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:50.990
It is because of
these experiences

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:53.750
that I wanted to honor John
with a concert featuring

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:56.090
a work of his choosing.

00:06:56.090 --> 00:06:59.540
So that was the original
intention of this program.

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:02.040
When I asked John
what he wanted to hear

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:05.600
he said without hesitation,
I'd love to hear some Dvorak.

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:06.530
And I said terrific.

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:09.200
How about the Te Deum
or the Stabat Mater?

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:10.370
He said no.

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:13.010
I want to hear The
Spectre's Bride.

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:16.580
And I said the who, what?

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:19.490
So here we are performing
this amazing piece

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:25.040
by Dvorak, which he described as
a dramatic musical enchantment.

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:27.890
Sadly, John passed
away last month.

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:29.870
But we decided to go
ahead and still think

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:33.530
of this as a tribute to John
and his incredible musical

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:36.890
contributions to
choral music at MIT.

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:38.750
A proper memorial
concert is being

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:42.980
planned for the fall of 2018.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.140
It seems appropriate
that at this concert

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:49.040
that I present the John
Oliver Choral Award.

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:53.720
This award is normally presented
only at our year end awards

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:56.930
ceremony of Music and
Theater Arts department,

00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:59.900
but I decided that this
was really the perfect time

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:03.440
to make sure that that
person was recognized.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:06.080
Thanks to the generous
donations of choir alums,

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:08.870
this is the fourth
year of the award.

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:11.330
Maestro Oliver requested
that the citation

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:15.260
read in recognition of a
choral student's dedication

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:18.590
to bringing another
aspect of his or her life

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:21.290
to fellow students
to the MIT community

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:25.370
at large through their
dedication to the choral art.

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:26.900
This student should
be a musician

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:30.080
of the highest caliber
with a passion for singing

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:33.260
and a model musical citizen.

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:35.929
I can think of no
one more deserving

00:08:35.929 --> 00:08:38.630
than Srini Raghuramam.

00:08:38.630 --> 00:08:42.320
Srini is currently a candidate
for the PhD program in Computer

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:45.410
Science with a music mind.

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:47.300
He is president of
the concert choir

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:50.900
and has sung with this
ensemble for five semesters.

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:52.550
He's also sung for
three semesters

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:54.740
with the MIT chamber
chorus, where

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:58.310
he's been a tenor soloist
in Bach's Mass in G Minor.

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:02.060
And most recently Benjamin
Britten's Rejoice in the Land.

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:05.203
This is his first year as
an Emerson vocal scholar,

00:09:05.203 --> 00:09:07.370
where he's studying he's
studying a voice with Kerry

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:08.720
Deal.

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:11.240
He's also sung as a
chorister for a production

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:15.620
of Tchaikovsky's opera the Maid
of Orleans with Odyssey opera.

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:19.100
And he's currently a member
of a special collegiate chorus

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:22.720
which will be singing with the
Boston Pops in a few weeks.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:25.550
Srini is also a gifted
composer and a teacher

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:28.490
of carnatic singing here at MIT.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:30.830
Besides his many
musical activities,

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:34.160
Srini has managed to help us
organize our recent choral tour

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:35.630
to Spain.

00:09:35.630 --> 00:09:39.320
And has been an invaluable
and unofficial assistant

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:42.160
to Kalina Schloneger
in our concerts office.

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:44.930
We literally could not have
done when we did this semester

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:46.640
without Srini.

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:50.300
And especially without his
enthusiastic, always cheerful,

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:51.980
and tireless help.

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:56.120
It gives me great pleasure to
present the 2018 John Oliver

00:09:56.120 --> 00:10:00.067
Choral Award to
Srini Raghuraman.

00:10:00.067 --> 00:10:03.560
[APPLAUSE AND CHEERS]

00:11:24.897 --> 00:11:28.390
[ORCHESTRA PLAYING]

00:15:09.954 --> 00:15:13.440
[SINGING IN CZECH]

01:33:48.354 --> 01:33:51.840
[APPLAUSE]

01:35:38.830 --> 01:35:42.700
Everyone, I personally
think this evening would not

01:35:42.700 --> 01:35:46.720
be complete if on behalf
of the concert choir

01:35:46.720 --> 01:35:50.260
I didn't say a few
words about Bill.

01:35:50.260 --> 01:35:55.120
So, there are simply no words
to accurately and adequately

01:35:55.120 --> 01:35:58.930
acknowledge and
appreciate Dr. Carter.

01:35:58.930 --> 01:36:02.170
Dr. Carter's passion
and dedication to music,

01:36:02.170 --> 01:36:06.790
to his peers, and to us,
the MIT concert choir.

01:36:06.790 --> 01:36:09.790
Endowed with talent
and humility alike,

01:36:09.790 --> 01:36:12.730
Dr. Carter makes for
an exceptional mentor,

01:36:12.730 --> 01:36:15.340
both musically and otherwise.

01:36:15.340 --> 01:36:19.000
I'm many times dumbstruck
by how Bill somehow manages

01:36:19.000 --> 01:36:21.490
to make every rehearsal fun.

01:36:21.490 --> 01:36:25.690
With time, I've begun to
understand this better.

01:36:25.690 --> 01:36:28.810
For Bill, it's very
much about the music

01:36:28.810 --> 01:36:30.910
but it's also about us.

01:36:30.910 --> 01:36:33.190
He understands us
and works with us.

01:36:33.190 --> 01:36:34.660
And we with him.

01:36:34.660 --> 01:36:36.370
We truly are a family.

01:36:36.370 --> 01:36:38.200
And this would be
harder to see if Bill

01:36:38.200 --> 01:36:40.220
weren't the director he is.

01:36:40.220 --> 01:36:44.470
This past semester has been a
different one, a demanding one.

01:36:44.470 --> 01:36:47.740
A special one for
many, many reasons.

01:36:47.740 --> 01:36:49.618
As we approach the
end of the semester,

01:36:49.618 --> 01:36:51.160
I would like to take
this opportunity

01:36:51.160 --> 01:36:53.650
to thank Bill on behalf
of the concert choir

01:36:53.650 --> 01:36:56.770
for getting us through it
with such strength and grace.

01:36:56.770 --> 01:36:58.000
For inspiring us.

01:36:58.000 --> 01:37:00.910
For being the Bill
we know and love.

01:37:00.910 --> 01:37:02.650
The MIT concert
choir would not be

01:37:02.650 --> 01:37:04.810
what it is without you, Bill.

01:37:04.810 --> 01:37:07.220
For that and so much more.

01:37:07.220 --> 01:37:09.250
We say thank you.

01:37:09.250 --> 01:37:12.900
[CHEERING]

